We study limit points of the spectral radii of $Aα$-matrices of graphs. Adapting a method used by J. B. Shearer in 1989, we prove a density property of $Aα$-limit points of caterpillars for $α$ close to zero. Precisely, we show that for $α∈[0,1/2)$ there exists a positive number $τ2​(α)>2$ such that any value $λ>τ2​(α)$ is an $Aα$-limit point. We also determine the existence of other intervals for which all its points are $Aα$-limit points. ... Our main result (Theorem 4.4) is that for any $α∈[0,1/2)$ there exists a positive number $τ2​(α)>2$ such that any value $λ>τ2​(α)$ is an $Aα$ -limit point. Additionally, we study (Theorem 5.1), for small values of $α$, the existence of intervals $[τ1​(α),τ1′​(α))$ for which all numbers are also $Aα$ -limit points.

Our main result, Theorem 4.4 generalizes Shearer’s result for the adjacency matrix to any $Aα$ matrix: For $0≤α<1/2$, we find a number $τ2​(α)$ so that if $λ∈[τ2​(α),+∞)$ then $λ$ is a limit point for the spectral radius of $Aα$. We determine intervals $I$ for which all numbers in $I$ are limit points for $Aα$, for $0≤α≤α∗:=3−27=0.226540919+$. ... For every connected graph $G$ with maximum vertex degree $Δ$, and for every $α∈[0,1]$, the $Aα$-spectral radius $ρAα​(G)$ satisfies the following properties:
(i) $12​(α​(Δ+1)+α2​(Δ+1)2+4​Δ​(1−2​α))⩽ρAα​(G)⩽Δ$;
(ii) if $H$ is a proper subgraph of $G$, then $ρAα​(H)<ρAα​(G)$;
(iii) if $0⩽α<β⩽1$, then $ρAα​(G)<ρAβ​(G)$. ... In [14] the authors proved that when $n→∞$ the spectral radius of the adjacency matrix of $T1,n,n$ converges to $2+5=2.058+$. ... This kind of construction provides a sequence of $Aα$ -limit points close to 2 and hopefully the smallest value for an interval of $Aα$ -limit points, at least when $α∼0$.
